LeetCode 116 — Populating Next Right Pointers in Each Node

From Brute Force → Most Optimal

All Approaches + Code + Explanation + Dry Run

1. Problem Statement

You are given a perfect binary tree.

Each node has:

val
left
right
next

Initially, every next pointer is:

null

Your task is to connect every node to the node immediately to its right on the same level.

For example:

        1
      /   \
     2     3
    / \   / \
   4   5 6   7

After connecting:

        1 → null


      2 → 3 → null


    4 → 5 → 6 → 7 → null
Important Observation

The problem specifically says Perfect Binary Tree.

That means:

Every internal node has exactly 2 children.
Every leaf is at the same level.

So:

        1
      /   \
     2     3
    / \   / \
   4   5 6   7

is perfect.

But:

        1
       /
      2

is not perfect.

This property allows us to create a very efficient O(1) extra-space solution.

Approach 1 — Brute Force
Idea

For every node, find the node immediately to its right at the same level.

One simple brute-force idea is:

Traverse the tree level by level.
Store every level in a list.
Connect adjacent nodes in that list.

This is essentially BFS, but we're explicitly storing each level.

Code
class Solution {


    public Node connect(Node root) {


        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }


        List<List<Node>> levels = new ArrayList<>();


        Queue<Node> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);


        while (!q.isEmpty()) {


            int size = q.size();


            List<Node> level = new ArrayList<>();


            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {


                Node curr = q.poll();


                level.add(curr);


                if (curr.left != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }


                if (curr.right != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }


            levels.add(level);
        }


        // Connect adjacent nodes
        for (List<Node> level : levels) {


            for (int i = 0; i < level.size() - 1; i++) {


                level.get(i).next = level.get(i + 1);
            }
        }


        return root;
    }
}

Complexity

Let N = number of nodes.

Time  = O(N)
Space = O(N)

The algorithm is correct, but we can do better in terms of auxiliary space.

Approach 2 — Level Order BFS ⭐

This is the first code you provided.

Idea

Use a queue.

For every level:

current → next → next → ... → null

When processing a node, the next node in the queue is the node immediately to its right within the same level.

So:

curr.next = q.peek();

But only if it isn't the last node of the level.

Your Code
class Solution {


    public Node connect(Node root) {


        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }


        Queue<Node> q = new LinkedList<>();


        q.offer(root);


        while (!q.isEmpty()) {


            int size = q.size();


            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {


                Node curr = q.poll();


                if (i < size - 1) {
                    curr.next = q.peek();
                }


                if (curr.left != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }


                if (curr.right != null) {
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }
        }


        return root;
    }
}

How q.peek() Works

Suppose the queue contains:

4 5 6 7

We process:

curr = 4

After removing 4:

5 6 7

So:

curr.next = q.peek();

becomes:

4.next = 5

Next:

curr = 5

Queue:

6 7

Therefore:

5.next = 6

And so on.

For 7:

i == size - 1

so we don't assign next.

It remains:

7.next = null
