Approach 3 — Recursive DFS ⭐

This is your second code.

The key observation is that because the tree is perfect, we know:

root.left

and

root.right

are siblings.

Therefore:

root.left.next = root.right;

But what about:

        1
      /   \
     2     3
    / \   / \
   4   5 6   7

We also need:

5 → 6

These nodes are not siblings.

But because the tree is perfect:

root.left.right

is immediately followed by:

root.right.left

Therefore:

root.left.right.next = root.right.left;

More generally:

root.left.next = root.right;

and:

root.right.next = root.next.left;

Your Recursive Code
class Solution {


    public Node connect(Node root) {


        if (root == null) {
            return root;
        }


        if (root.left != null) {


            root.left.next = root.right;
        }


        if (root.right != null && root.next != null) {


            root.right.next = root.next.left;
        }


        connect(root.left);
        connect(root.right);


        return root;
    }
}

Important Line

This is the most important line:

root.right.next = root.next.left;

Let's understand it.

Suppose:

        1 → null
      /       \
     2  ─────→ 3
    / \       / \
   4   5     6   7

For node 2:

root = 2
root.next = 3

Therefore:

root.right.next = root.next.left;

means:

5.next = 3.left;

which gives:

5.next = 6

Exactly what we need.

