Complexity

Every node is visited once.

Time = O(N)

The recursion stack depends on the height.

For a perfect binary tree:

Height = O(log N)

Therefore:

Space = O(log N)

This is already much better than BFS's O(N) auxiliary space.

Approach 4 — Iterative O(1) Extra Space ⭐⭐⭐ Most Optimal

We can go one step further.

Instead of recursion, we can use the already-created next pointers to move horizontally across the current level.

This eliminates:

Queue
Recursion stack

So we get:

Time  = O(N)
Space = O(1)
Idea

At each level:

        1
      /   \
     2     3

First connect:

2 → 3

Then move to the next level.

Now:

2 → 3

allows us to traverse the current level without a queue.

For every node:

current.left.next = current.right

And if there is a next node:

current.right.next = current.next.left

Most Optimal Code
class Solution {


    public Node connect(Node root) {


        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }


        Node leftmost = root;


        while (leftmost.left != null) {


            Node curr = leftmost;


            while (curr != null) {


                // Connect left child to right child
                curr.left.next = curr.right;


                // Connect right child to next node's left child
                if (curr.next != null) {
                    curr.right.next = curr.next.left;
                }


                // Move horizontally
                curr = curr.next;
            }


            // Move to next level
            leftmost = leftmost.left;
        }


        return root;
    }
}

