Approach Selection
🟢 Approach 1 — Brute Force

Good for understanding the problem.

Level → store nodes → connect adjacent nodes

But unnecessary extra storage.

🟢 Approach 2 — BFS

Very easy to understand and implement.

curr.next = q.peek();

This is probably the easiest solution in an interview.

Time  = O(N)
Space = O(N)

🟡 Approach 3 — Recursive

Uses the special property of a perfect binary tree:

root.left.next = root.right;


root.right.next = root.next.left;
Time  = O(N)
Space = O(log N)

🟢 Approach 4 — Iterative O(1)

Best solution.

Current level:
next pointers → traverse horizontally


Children:
create next level's next pointers
Time  = O(N)
Space = O(1)

🧠 Pattern to Remember

For LeetCode 116, remember these two connections:

Connection 1 — Same Parent
        parent
       /      \
      L        R

Always:

L.next = R;
Connection 2 — Different Parents
        A → B
       / \   / \
      L   R X   Y

Connect:

R.next = X;

In terms of pointers:

R.next = A.next.left;

So the entire solution boils down to:

root.left.next = root.right;


if (root.next != null) {
    root.right.next = root.next.left;
}

And the most optimal implementation simply performs those connections iteratively without a queue or recursion.

116 = Perfect Binary Tree + next pointer connection + O(1) space.
